# Work-based commit messages

Every git commit (including when pushing to live / GitHub) **must** describe the work that was done. This is mandatory for every agent.

## Required format

```
type(scope): short description of what changed [CHG-YYYY-DDMM-NNN]
```

- Use the Change ID from this request when one exists (`AGENTS.md`).
- The subject must be specific to **this** change (what improved, fixed, or shipped).
- When the user asks to push / go live / push all changes: draft that message, then run `npm run push:live -- "<message>"` immediately. Do not use a generic message.

## Forbidden (never use)

- `updates changelogs DDMMYYYY` (alone or as the whole subject)
- Vague subjects: `updates`, `changes`, `wip`, `fix stuff`, `misc`, `misc updates`
- Reusing the previous commit message when the work is different
- Omitting the Change ID when a CHG-* id was created for this work

## Push to live

```bash
npm run push:live -- "type(scope): short description of the work [CHG-YYYY-DDMM-NNN]"
```

`push:live` **requires** that message argument (or `PUSH_LIVE_MESSAGE`) and refuses generic `updates changelogs DDMMYYYY` subjects.

## Checklist before `git commit` / `push:live`

1. What did this change do for the user or system? Put that in the subject.
2. Include `[CHG-…]` when applicable.
3. If the subject would still make sense for an unrelated PR, it is too vague — rewrite.
